n you want to  explore all the solutions so that's why  at the beginning i said it's not just  black box optimization  that i'm interested in here i'm  interested in black box  exploration to discover  new solutions to problems we have  all right  so so what is a g flow net it's it's a  fairly  new way of thinking um  uh  about learning how to  generate something how to sample  a configuration that makes sense if you  want to  generate a an object that's  compositional that you know you could  construct that object through a sequence  of steps  you can think of g phonetics as a  particular kind of reinforcement  learning methods  uh but instead of maximizing which is  what  rl is trying to do which it may not  produce as much diversity as we want  is trying to sample in proportion to the  reward  so  the mathematical formalism here  uh considers all the possible ways that  you could generate  um an x so we think of an x as an object  like a molecule  or a sequence of words  um that we can generate through a  sequence of steps  and there may be many ways of producing  the same query the same x the same  question corresponding to different  paths in these sequence of actions  so that's what the  directed a secret graph on this  um slide represents  and  mathematically we think of it like in a  flow network you think of like water  flowing from a root node which is where  you know we always start from  and ending up in a sink note and  learning the g-flow net amounts to  finding how much water should go in all  of the arcs there  okay  so  there is something  uh more general here than what i'm  talking about this  idea of converting  assembling machinery like monte carlo  mark of chains that doesn't involve  machine learning as such  into a problem in training  like a deep net that can do the java  sampling  and  you know the reason why  people have not been using mcmc in many  cases as i said because  sampling might take just too much time  to get good samples uh exponentially  exponentially long time in fact  so you know is there any reason to think  that this approach of  learning to sample  which we call an amortized generator  might work better  in principle if the distribution that we  are trying to sample from is arbitrarily  complicated then there is no hope that  we can do better  but if there is structure like these  modes  for example here  you see you have these three modes three  regions where the function we we are  interested in is high  and and the learner has already found  examples from these three regions  but now  it needs to explore and and kind of  guess other places where the function  might be high that haven't been visited  yet  so  in principle in a high dimensional space  like the number of places you could try  is so huge that this is a hopeless thing  that's what i mean by intractable and  exponential time  but if those modes are arranged in a way  that is structured and the learning  algorithm can discover that structure  then we are in business then we can turn  something that looks intractable into  something that is tractable  so in the example here which is of  course a toy example you can see that  the three modes we have discovered line  up on a grid  on three of these intersections  and so the learner might guess oh maybe  there is something on this fourth  intersection that you know  we haven't visited yet so so that's what  i mean um by  um  you know taking advantage of the power  of generalization in particular this is  a form of what we call  system materialization researchers in ai  these days are very excited about how do  we generalize  far from the data in a way that's  meaningful  okay  so  in general there is no free lunch but  here  we could be lucky if the  underlying  function that we're trying to discover  has a structure  has regularities that machine learning  can take advantage of which is why we're  doing machine learning on the data in  the first place  then we might be able to  produce those samples to explore the  environment to explore potential  solutions  much more efficiently than if we use the  traditional  and cmc methods okay  so so that's for motivation  um now  my team and i have been working on the  mathematical foundations of  this this g-flowness um and i'm i'm not  going to explain uh all that in in much  detail  um  except to say that um it's related to  reinforcement learning as i said  um but it uses a slightly different  formalism  um where we think of these trajectories  as the  objects of interest for uh probability  uh modeling  and  by thinking about it in this way we come  up inspired by this analogy with flows  in a flow network  we came up with  conditions and a training objective  that will guarantee that we can if we  train a function that predicts how much  flow should go through each edge and  each node of this  um flow network  that this function will  correspond to a proper flow  and the condition is very simple and  intuitive it says that the amount of  flow coming into  a node s prime here  which corresponds to an intermediate  stage in the construction of a candidate  um  is equal to the amount of flow coming  out to all the possible next states  and we can design an objective function  sorry here  there's an objective function which  basically is penalizes for the mismatch  between the amount of flow coming in the  state as t and the amount of flow coming  out of a state st so the f hat here is a  new net is something you can train and  and the  loss function right here is something  you know we can compute the derivatives  of so we can train that neural net to  predict how much flow should be going  through each edge  um and then we can prove that  if we can train that neural net properly  then  it has the right property that  if you  um if you travel in the flow network  according to with  at each stage  making a transition to the next state  with a probability that is proportional  to the flow  on the outgoing edge  so  that's the conditional probability of  going to some next state given the  previous state  um  then what you get are samples  that show up with probability that's  what you want that is equal to the  reward uh with normalized right so  proportional to the reward function  so that that was the that's the main uh  property here that we wanted  um but but then like  unexpectedly it turns out that this  formalism also opens the door to many  other goodies for  probabilistic modeling uh in particular  um  if we make these flow networks are  conditional  we can use them for  calculating things that seem intractable  sums over an exponential number of  possible  candidates for example  if  let's say  we think of a candidate like a graph or  a sequence  and we ask  well what's the probability of all the  graphs or all the sequences that are  continuations of that sequence or that  graph that extensions of that sequence  or that graph  um well it turns out we with the neural  net we can get an answer  uh by a simple call to the neural net  um so so we can we can do what's called  marginalization or compute what's called  free energies  and with that we can compute  things that  people um you know find very difficult  in principle to compute  uh like entropies and mutual  informations  um now of course  again you know going back to this slide  about the free lunch  um this is assuming we can train the  neural net which  uh assumes that well we have enough  compute power enough  enough um  a big enough network  uh but also that there is structure that  the  underlying function we're trying to  capture  enables generalization  and in that case  we would be able to compute all these  goodies  um  yeah let me let me uh also mention  something  practical which is  you know going beyond the application to  converting  a reward function or an energy function  into a generative model  one question i guess i get is well but  what if i don't know the energy function  the reward function i only have data  right  so so then what you could do  is train the energy function  and and  in principle we know how to do that  there is a classical recipe for  computing the gradient in an energy  based model  which is the equation that's written  there  and the problem with that equation is  that it requires  sampling from  the distribution that corresponds to the  energy function and in general we can  use monte carlo markov chain mcmc to do  that but it's intractable it doesn't  work very well and so people don't use  this approach  but now if we are able to sample from  the distribution at least approximately  corresponding to the energy function we  could train the energy function in other  words if you give me some data  uh x um  i can and in our case this would be the  input output pairs x and y or it could  be you know any any kind of data that we  want to model  um  this approach uh would allow to learn a  joint distribution an energy function  between those variables  um taking advantage of the sampling  capability  of the g-flow net  now let me bring up  a  an ingredient that i haven't really  discussed much up to now  i said at the beginning  that we would like  to  query  configurations experiments  that are going to give us a lot of  information so what does that mean  and and i talk about uncertainty and  error right so where does my predictor  make errors  so  the  total error that that a a supervised  learning predictor is making  can be decomposed into two parts one we  call  epistemic uncertainty and the other  aliatoric uncertainty so these are big  words to say two things that are pretty  straightforward so the alliatoric  uncertainty is the part of the  prediction error  which has nothing to do with our model  it just has to do with the fact that  there's some in predictability in the  data like you know there is only so much  the best you could do even if you were  um  if you knew the true  model of the data the true uh generative  distribution you would still make errors  because there is noise right so  electronic concern d is just noise  there's unpredictability  and we can't do anything about this in a  way we want to you know kind of ignore  it here  when we're trying to acquire information  about  um an environment about our our world  um  we care about epistemic uncertainty so  that's the rest that's the part of the  error that we're making  which is due to our learner due to the  fact that our learner didn't have enough  data  uh that it didn't have enough capacity  that we didn't train it enough or  whatever  okay so that's epistemic uncertainty  epistemic you know has to do with  knowledge  so there's been a lot of work in machine  learning to trying to estimate epistemic  at something if you train a machine  learning predictor  how much confidence can you have in it  or actually more precisely  uh what part of that confidence can you  reduce  in particular you would like to know the  epistemic uncertainty  at a particular point to because those  points where you have a lot of  uncertainty  uh you might want to query so this is  illustrated in the figure here  and this is taken from um  standard  uh discussions uh around what's called  bayesian optimization  which are about  this  question of  where we should query  if  we can estimate the uncertainty the  epistemic uncertainty so one way to  estimate epistemic uncertainty is the  bayesian approach where we say  well  really it's not just like there's one  predictor that could fit the data  there's a whole lot of predictors that  could flip the data so in the figure we  have these three data points in red  and the blue sausage  represents all of the functions we think  could fit the data and are consistent  with our prior  and maybe the the  average of them is the blue  prediction  but maybe the true one is the which we  don't know of course is the uh black  dotted thing  so  you know where the sausages is wide uh  is is probably a good place to query  that's what we're saying and and then we  can use different methods to estimate  the width of that sausage which is the  which could be for example the variance  of our  basic variance of that predictor uh at  that point  okay so we've been applying these ideas  in the context of discovering new  molecules  um  and um and it seems to work quite well  so we've compared with mcnc methods  we've compared with reinforcement  learning method in particular what's  called ppo  and  what these graphs show is that  we are able to discover these high  scoring molecules  faster than than the more standard  methods but more importantly the  diversity of the solutions that we're  getting is much larger  um so in the middle we have the number  of modes found and that's kind of a good  metric of diversity like number of  really different solutions that are  found and you can see  the um bold  solutions which is using our g-flow net  approach that  rises up to covering more of the modes  faster  so so that's really exciting um and and  i think that  is it's just the uh beginning of um  a way to apply machine learning that's  more focused on  how the learner interacts with uh their  environment  um  and how that changes the dynamic of uh  you know how we design uh these these  machine learning systems the classical  act of learning that i've been talking  about  is good but but it doesn't scale to  those complex spaces where the the  queries the x the inputs are  high dimensional and complex so  this is  what  the approach that i talked about here  really enables  and um you know i'd be happy to answer  questions um either  uh later or you know  through email so thanks for your  attention
 

